    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Riemann's rearrangement theorem\n\nIf $\\sum a_n$ converges **conditionally** (not absolutely), then for every $S \\in \\mathbb{R} \\cup \\{\\pm\\infty\\}$ there is a permutation $\\sigma$ such that $\\sum a_{\\sigma(n)} = S$. Re-ordering changes the sum.\n\nDemonstration: take the alternating harmonic series and rearrange so two positive terms appear before each negative term. The new arrangement converges to $\\tfrac{3}{2}\\ln 2$, not $\\ln 2$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Rearranged: 1 + 1/3 - 1/2 + 1/5 + 1/7 - 1/4 + 1/9 + 1/11 - 1/6 + ... */\nM_groups : 200$\nrearr_partial : []$\nrunning : 0.0$\npos_idx : 0$ neg_idx : 0$\nfor k : 1 thru M_groups do block([],\n  /* Two positive odd-denominator terms */\n  running : running + 1.0 / (2*pos_idx + 1),  pos_idx : pos_idx + 1,\n  rearr_partial : endcons(running, rearr_partial),\n  running : running + 1.0 / (2*pos_idx + 1),  pos_idx : pos_idx + 1,\n  rearr_partial : endcons(running, rearr_partial),\n  /* One negative even-denominator term */\n  running : running - 1.0 / (2*(neg_idx + 1)),  neg_idx : neg_idx + 1,\n  rearr_partial : endcons(running, rearr_partial))$\n\nprint(\"rearranged sum after\", length(rearr_partial), \"terms ≈\", last(rearr_partial))$\nprint(\"(3/2) ln(2) ≈\", float(3*log(2)/2))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 21,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "rearranged sum after 600 terms ≈ 1.0384719427121702\n(3/2) ln(2) ≈ 1.0397207708399179"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "So the same set of terms, in a different order, converges to a different number. The lesson: **for conditionally convergent series, the order matters.** Absolute convergence is what guarantees you can rearrange freely.\n\n## What we got\n\n- Convergence is about partial sums, not individual terms.\n- Standard tests (ratio, root, integral, comparison) handle most series.\n- Conditional convergence is a fragile thing — robustness comes from absolute convergence.\n- The integral test and ratio test are direct stepping stones to power series (next notebook).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}